@meta
id: 18-05
title: Create a file where you are standing
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: netrw
par: 150
minutes: 5
vscode: right-click the folder, New File
open: src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts

@brief
The customer module has no barrel file. Every import into it spells out
`customer/customer.entity` and `customer/customer.provider`, and the
team want `src/domain/customer/index.ts` so callers can import from
`../customer` instead.

Create that file, from the browser, without typing its directory. It
needs exactly these three lines:

    export * from './customer.entity';
    export * from './customer.provider';
    export * from './customer.repository';

`:Explore` puts you in the customer directory. In a netrw listing, `%`
asks for a file name, creates it in the directory you are looking at,
and opens it. `d` does the same for a directory. `D` deletes and `R`
renames the entry under the cursor; you do not need either today.

@keys
| `:Ex` | browse the current file's directory |
| `%` | new file here — netrw asks for the name |
| `d` | new directory here |
| `D` `R` | delete, rename the entry under the cursor |

@hints
`:Ex` first. The listing's header shows which directory you are in;
that is where `%` will create the file.
--
Press `%`, type `index.ts`, Enter. You are now editing an empty buffer
at the right path. `i`, the three lines, `Esc`, `:wq`.
--
`:Ex` Enter, `%index.ts` Enter, `i`, the three `export` lines, `Esc`,
`:wq`. Nothing else.

@solution
    :Ex<Enter> %index.ts<Enter>
    i export * from './customer.entity'; ...(three lines)... <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`:e src/domain/customer/index.ts` would have done it too, and with Tab
completion it is not many more keys. `%` earns its keep when you are
already looking at the directory, which is the normal case: you browsed
there to check what exists, saw what was missing, and made it on the
spot.

The typing dominates the count here, as it will whenever a lesson has
you write a file. Par assumes you type the three lines once.

@start src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

@start src/domain/customer/customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
};

@start src/domain/customer/customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
      name: record.name,
    };
  }
}

@expected src/domain/customer/index.ts
export * from './customer.entity';
export * from './customer.provider';
export * from './customer.repository';
